  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2)$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =7-\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne 2$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is $(\\,2,\\infty \\,).$",
